evidence.quote:
The intended task contract should name:
- parent logical operation ID and total deadline;
- input validation and authorization state;
- which child results must join before responding;
- response, error and cancellation propagation;
- timeout/abort behavior and connection/lock release;
- whether effects are reversible, committed or require reconciliation;
- maximum queue/concurrency budget per dependency.

- the job or workflow identity;
- the scheduled time or logical window;
- the owner/tenant and configuration revision where relevant.

A modernization path usually has several states:

- legacy only;
- dual write: both paths receive writes, with a reconciliation process;
- shadow: the target receives traffic but does not own the authoritative response;
- cutover: the target owns the authoritative response;
- rollback: traffic returns to the legacy path while the target drains.

- success rate 1.0 (the modeled traffic is served);
- maximum divergence 8,925.5 records (95% CI 5,561.6-12,289.3);
- rollback completed 0.1: it finishes in only 10% of replications;

The V12 modernization model represents the states as a stage schedule and a set of stocks:

- dual_write_backlog: records awaiting reconciliation;
- divergence: accumulated observed difference between paths;
- legacy_share and target_share: traffic allocation;
- rollback timer: time since the rollback trigger.

The model records:

- whether the rollback completed within the horizon;
- the time from trigger to drain, or a censored value at the horizon;
- the maximum divergence observed before the trigger.

A cutover record could be decision-supported only with:

- a measured dual-write lag with a bounded upper confidence limit;
- a measured reconciliation capacity in the target environment;
- a rehearsed rollback that completes inside the observation window;
- a measured divergence below the business threshold across a sustained period;
- a confirmed continuity and reconciliation runbook.

Before a transition changes authoritative state or retirements become irreversible, require evidence for:

- named change owner and approved versions/assets;
- old/new client × API × data compatibility matrix, including asynchronous readers;
- no overlapping uncoordinated writers;
- tested retry identity and late acknowledgment handling;
- representative user-level integrity/authorization tests;
- source-to-target reconciliation and residual unknowns;
- recovery point, actual restored data validity, maximum permissible loss and time;
- explicit stop/hold mechanism if monitoring misses a fault;
- decommissioning evidence for all old callers/subscriptions.

Even an apparently harmless read-index migration needs to compare *semantics*, not only row totals:

- stable entity/tenant identity and per-object access filters;
- deletions, revocations, updates that cross transaction boundaries and repeated events;
- source revision and index-generation checkpoint;
- replay order, deduplication, late arrivals and incomplete snapshot coverage;
- normal and degraded freshness; clocks and unknown timestamps;
- query modes (identifier, exact match, prefix, full-text) and pagination/ordering guarantees;
- matching visible results under old/new client versions;
- measured cost and operations required to re-index after failure.

Convergence does not mean:

- the model is valid;
- the inputs are measured;
- the decision is correct;
- the confidence interval contains the production value;
- more replications will fix a biased model.
